FreeBSD-SA-26:81.vfs                                        Security Advisory
                                                          The FreeBSD Project

Topic:          Capsicum and O_RESOLVE_BENEATH bypass at mount roots

Category:       core
Module:         vfs
Announced:      2026-11-17
Credits:        Pawel Dawidek
Credits:        Lexi Winter
Affects:        FreeBSD 15.1
Corrected:      2026-11-17 17:06:00 UTC (stable/15, 15.2-STABLE)
                2026-11-17 17:08:16 UTC (releng/15.1, 15.1-RELEASE-p7)
CVE Name:       CVE-2026-101312

For general information regarding FreeBSD Security Advisories,
including descriptions of the fields above, security branches, and the
following sections, please visit <URL:https://security.FreeBSD.org/>.

I.   Background

Capsicum is a capability and sandbox framework.  A process in capability mode
can only access files through paths relative to directory descriptors it
already holds, using openat(2) and the other *at() system calls.  The kernel
confines such lookups beneath the starting directory, failing any ".."
component that would leave the starting directory with ENOTCAPABLE.  The same
confinement applies outside capability mode to directory descriptors limited
with cap_rights_limit(2), and to lookups using the O_RESOLVE_BENEATH or
AT_RESOLVE_BENEATH flags.

By default, all such lookups can use a ".." component that stays beneath the
starting directory, for example "sub/..".  The vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot sysctl
controls this behavior in capability mode and for capability-limited
directory descriptors.

II.  Problem Description

To resolve ".." from the root of a mounted file system, the kernel first
steps to the directory on which that file system is mounted, then looks up
".." there.  The check for leaving the starting directory compared against
the mount point rather than the root of the mounted file system.  If the
starting directory was itself the root of a mounted file system, the check
never matched, and the lookup escaped to the parent of the mount point.

III. Impact

A sandboxed process, or a program relying on O_RESOLVE_BENEATH or
AT_RESOLVE_BENEATH, whose starting directory is the root of a mounted file
system can access any file under its root directory.  The descriptor's rights
and normal file system permissions still apply.  Lookups that start in any
other directory are not affected.

IV.  Workaround

No complete workaround is available.  Setting the vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot
sysctl to 0 refuses all ".." components in capability mode and for
capability-limited descriptors:

# sysctl vfs.lookup_cap_dotdot=0

This also refuses components that stay beneath the starting directory, such
as "sub/..", which some applications need, and has no effect on
O_RESOLVE_BENEATH or AT_RESOLVE_BENEATH lookups outside capability mode.

V.   Solution

Upgrade your vulnerable system to a supported FreeBSD stable or
release / security branch (releng) dated after the correction date,
and reboot the system.

Perform one of the following:

1) To update your vulnerable system installed from base system packages:

Systems running a 15.0-RELEASE or later version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or
arm64 platforms, which were installed using base system packages, can be
updated via the pkg(8) utility:

# pkg upgrade -r FreeBSD-base
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for a security update"

2) To update your vulnerable system installed from binary distribution sets:

Systems running a RELEASE version of FreeBSD on the amd64 or arm64 platforms
which were not installed using base system packages can be updated via the
freebsd-update(8) utility:

# freebsd-update fetch
# freebsd-update install
# shutdown -r +10min "Rebooting for a security update"

3) To update your vulnerable system via a source code patch:

The following patches have been verified to apply to the applicable
FreeBSD release branches.

a) Download the relevant patch from the location below, and verify the
detached PGP signature using your PGP utility.

# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:81/vfs.patch
# fetch https://security.FreeBSD.org/patches/SA-26:81/vfs.patch.asc
# gpg --verify vfs.patch.asc

b) Apply the patch.  Execute the following commands as root:

# cd /usr/src
# patch -E -p0 < /path/to/patch

c) Recompile your kernel as described in
<URL:https://www.FreeBSD.org/handbook/kernelconfig.html> and reboot the
system.

VI.  Correction details

This issue is corrected as of the corresponding Git commit hash in the
following stable and release branches:

Branch/path                             Hash                     Revision
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------
stable/15/                              9b7c7bcebd4d    stable/15-n286871
releng/15.1/                            c0de320f18f0  releng/15.1-n283674
- -------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run the following command to see which files were modified by a
particular commit:

# git show --stat <commit hash>

Or visit the following URL, replacing NNNNNN with the hash:

<URL:https://cgit.freebsd.org/src/commit/?id=NNNNNN>

To determine the commit count in a working tree (for comparison against
nNNNNNN in the table above), run:

# git rev-list --count --first-parent HEAD

